<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khanate of Sibir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century–<a href="/years/1598">1598</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sibir Khanate, Seber qannïq, Sibirskoye khanstvo, Sibirsky yurt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khanate of Sibir was a Siberian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> state in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, founded at the end of the 15th century after the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. The Shaybanid and Taibugid dynasties often contested its rule, and its people included Siberian Tatars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanty">Khanty</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansi">Mansi</a> and Selkup; it was the northernmost Muslim state in recorded history. Its capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyumen">Chimgi-Tura</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskar">Iskar</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a>. The Shaybanid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuchum">Kuchum</a> took power after fighting from 1556 to 1563, but a raid on the Stroganov trading posts brought an expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> under Yermak, who defeated him in 1582. Kuchum fled to the Nogai in 1598.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquest of the <b>Khanate of Sibir</b> in the late sixteenth century opened the fur trade that financed further eastward expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the cossack leader Yermak Timofeyevich attacked the <b>Khanate of Sibir</b> in 1582, Russian traders, cossacks and soldiers advanced across Siberia along its rivers, founding Tobolsk, Tomsk and Yakutsk, and reached the Pacific at the Sea of Okhotsk in 1639.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
